{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q003_actin_like_atpase_divergence","answer":"SCOPe 2.08 places the catalytic (nucleotide-binding) domains of actin/HSP70, hexokinase, and glycerol kinase in the same superfamily, c.55.1 "Actin-like ATPase domain" (sunid 53067), but in three distinct families: c.55.1.1 Actin/HSP70 (sunid 53068), c.55.1.3 Hexokinase (sunid 53083), and c.55.1.4 Glycerol kinase (sunid 53089). The superfamily sits in fold c.55 "Ribonuclease H-like motif" (sunid 53066; 3 layers a/b/a, mixed 5-stranded sheet, order 32145, strand 2 antiparallel) within class c (alpha and beta proteins, a/b). The superfamily carries the curated note "duplication: contains two domains of this fold", so each of these proteins has two tandem RNase H-like lobes with the nucleotide bound in the cleft between them; SCOPe splits each chain into two c.55.1 domains.

What the relationship permits: (1) Superfamily-level membership is SCOPe's assertion of probable common ancestry (homology) based on shared structure and shared functional features, here the conserved ATP/ADP-binding site geometry, the phosphate-binding motifs, and the two-lobe duplicated architecture, despite low or undetectable pairwise sequence identity. It is therefore legitimate to infer that actin, HSP70, hexokinase, and glycerol kinase descend from one ancestral duplicated ATPase and share the same nucleotide-binding mechanism and interdomain-closure mode. (2) Separate family placement means SCOPe found no clear sequence-level evidence of close relationship (families typically require >30% identity or very similar structure and function). The three are remote homologues, not close ones.

What it does NOT permit: Superfamily membership does not license transfer of specific function, substrate specificity, or cellular role. Actin is a polymerizing cytoskeletal ATPase, HSP70 is a chaperone whose ATPase domain allosterically controls a separate substrate-binding domain, hexokinase phosphorylates glucose, and glycerol kinase phosphorylates glycerol; these roles are family- or protein-specific and diverged after the shared ancestor. Substrate-binding residues, regulatory sites, and oligomeric behaviour cannot be transferred across families on the basis of c.55.1 membership. Likewise, membership in the broader fold c.55 (shared with RNase H-like nucleases c.55.3, creatinase N-domain c.55.2, MutS domain II c.55.6, etc.) implies only a common topology, not homology, so no evolutionary or functional inference should be drawn between the actin-like ATPases and, for example, RNase H or integrase. Using one of these proteins as a modelling template for another is reasonable for the core sheet and nucleotide site but unreliable for the lobe-specific insertions and the substrate-binding surfaces. Any stronger claim (e.g., that a new sequence belongs to one of these families) would require independent evidence such as sequence profile/HMM hits or structural alignment, not just the shared SCOPe superfamily.","evidence_used":["mcp__scope__get_scop_node","mcp__scope__list_scop_children","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/c.55.1","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/c.55.1/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/c.55.1/children","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/c.55/children","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/53067/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/53066/annotations"]}]}
